castle point and rochard health and  wellbeing strategy reset  community resilience workshop from the  9th of september 2020  facilitated by sarah mills who is a  locality  development manager at castle point and  rochford clinical commissioning group  scene setting for this new priority area  the 2020 code 19 pandemic  has presented unprecedented challenges  as to how we live together as residents  and work together in our communities  district and borough councils were asked  to mobilize residents  and join up with key community leaders  and partners to provide a response to  meet the needs  of the most vulnerable in our  communities this challenge was met both  as a county and as districts  and the data from operation shield  presented on the following slides  reflects that response this pandemic has  again  highlighted the importance of our  community's ability to respond to change  to care for each other and themselves  and to be connected into wider networks  at times of difficulty  this community resilience has been an  underlying theme in much of the  system-wide thinking about new ways of  working at a more local level  this is highlighted in work such as the  primary care networks and e-put  community health services delivery model  to support integration and deliver new  ways of locality working  based on population health and local  need  these developments have created the  appetite across the ball to ensure that  this is a new distinct area for  consideration  and a standalone theme in our new health  and wellbeing strategy  questions to consider as you look at the  data one  what does the data tell us two what are  the key themes identified  three what is the current situation and  what might the near future look like for  residents given the effects of covert 19  has had on community resilience sarah  mills commented saying  obviously the data set that we have for  community resilience is somewhat limited  because it's a new priority area we  don't have that historical collection of  data  also it's much more difficult to  quantify community resilience is a  little bit more intangible  qualitative and a bit more about  perceptions than facts  operations shield what the data tells us  the  data collected by essex county council  as part of operation shield tells us  the number of extremely vulnerable or  category a residents  there are in each district and borough  how many of these had registered on the  government portal to indicate what  needs they had how many people sought  help from the essex welfare service and  local community hubs during the  coronavirus pandemic  the type of help requested for example  with getting food  medicines befriending or healthy  lifestyle advice  and where in each district and borough  their requests came from by ward  and an age breakdown operation shield  summary headlines  a large volume of support has been  offered to residents across essex  by the essex welfare service and local  community hubs in each district  a total of 889 people have contacted the  community hubs to date  in castle point and rochford 319  and 570 respectively  861 contacts have been made to the essex  welfare service across castle point and  rockford  577 and 284 respectively  75 referrals were made from the essex  welfare service the community hub in  castle point  and 39 were made to the rochford hub  this data was taken from essex county  council operations shield dashboard  requests for help in castle point of the  4727 extremely vulnerable or category a  residents in castle point  2763 or 58.5  had registered on the government portal  to indicate what needs they had  the most requests for help came from  james's ward  of the 75 referrals from essex welfare  service to the community hub  68 were for food one was for befriending  and six for other support the majority  of these 75 referrals were for people  aged 65 to 74.  sarah mills commented on this saying i  think there's that understanding that we  know who some of these people  are but we don't know who all of them  are  requests for help in the rocha district  after 5737 extremely vulnerable or  category a residence in the rocha  district  3246 or 57  were registered on the government portal  to indicate what needs they  had the most request for help came from  trinity ward  of the 39 referrals from essex welfare  service to the rochford community hub  35 were for food two referrals worth of  medicine  and two for other support 15 of the 39  referrals were for people aged 75 plus  14 four people aged 65 to 74 years  and 10 for 18 to 64 years  sarah mills asked are those unregistered  vulnerable people really difficult to  find  to help and to offer support to  referrals to essex welfare service  a total of 9211 contacts have been made  to the essex welfare service today  across the whole of essex  of these 577 have come from castle point  and 284 from rochford a breakdown by  ward shows that the greatest number of  referrals within castle point came from  canvay island north  in the rochwood district the greatest  number of referrals came from hockley  help with getting food was the main  reason for referral in both areas  a breakdown by age ban shows that the  majority of contacts made the essex  welfare service  from both castle point and rockford  where residents aged 75 plus  discussion reflecting on the data sarah  mills asked the group to share their  faults in response to their  experience over the last few months of  the community and voluntary offer  locally  and whether the data looked familiar to  those present  discussion points scott williamson  discussed that  isolation and vulnerability among people  is very evident  who don't feel able for whatever reason  to connect into the service we have in  our community  i'd love to know why that is julie  salford said  lots of older people believe they do not  need help and would prefer  services to go to people that genuinely  need it  sarah mills explained that the total  offer is greater than the sum of its  parts  she also commented saying we need to  make sure everyone has a voice and the  needs that they recognise were as  important as health needs  and social care needs scott williamson  commented saying  i think the key thing for us is we  worked in partnership  that was really helpful we had the  council we had the community  we had churches we had volunteers i  think that was really powerful  victoria mazuki added the discussion  saying we would not have had the  success of the hub and helped the people  if it was not a partnership approach  we couldn't have done it ourselves the  partnership is the strength  sarah mills concluded this part of the  conversation by saying  so in terms of the strategy one of the  essential tenets of community resilience  is partnership working regarding the  essex county council data showing  unemployment levels due to covid and  furlough  councillor webb commented in rochford  we've not had that level of unemployment  before  and maybe that feeds into people not  wanting to accept help because as a  district we've not been in that position  before  whereas other parts of the county have  been in that position before  and yet now we've got that same issue to  some extent in rochefort  i think that's where the community  struggles to adapt to being in a  different  place to where it has been before  sarah mills asked how do you think  that's going to impact on what the  community needs from not  only its statutory services but also its  voluntary services  victoria mazuki responded saying future  work has  got to be food banks as they're not the  norm what can we do to build people  through budgeting  apprenticeships offering them  volunteering and upskilling  so that they have the ability to find  work when it's available  and what can we do to support them with  for example cooking courses to be able  to make them not reliant on food banks  scott williamson commented saying what  we're looking at is how do we train  people  how can we help people out of this  crisis going forward what are the needs  that come up  why do people go to the food banks we're  looking to put a program together to  deliver on some of that  he also added that it would be  beneficial from both sides to linking  with some of the partners of local gp  practices  in the southeast essex locality strategy  the overall system ambition is to  improve the well-being and lives of the  people we serve focus on prevention and  supporting the strengths of communities  and individuals  prioritize the needs and locations of  people rather than the boundaries of  organizations  and work with each other and the local  population to organize services  and mobilize resources within  communities  underlying principles agreed for south  east essex are as follows  locality first thinking driving this at  an organizational level  population health management which  includes prevention personal  empowerment and community resilience  co-production which will facilitate  local people  to be involved in all levels of  developing implementing  reviewing and assessing the new models  of care sharing of resource  which will implement and support the  wide transformation of the system  and communications and engagement which  are system-wide and cross-locality  our ambition for locality leadership  sarah mills then presented the vision  for locality leadership that has come  out of conversation with a variety of  healthcare partners and others  the overall ambition is to have leaders  from community organizations  alongside residents and faith leaders  committed to improving health and  well-being  as well as health and social care  workers and mental health practitioners  who together have a collective  leadership function the leadership  function is as follows  identifying and sharing a common purpose  reviewing current collaborative working  engineering opportunities to build  multi-agency relationships  building the foundations for a locality  integrated network  reviewing data and identifying  priorities identifying workforce gaps  and development needs  creating a culture of quality  improvement and initial  signal point of escalation for  partnership working  sarah mills then explained that this  locality leadership would direct the  strategy and formalize partnership  working  recognising that the work that the  community sector can do  can happen more upstream and prevent  some of those crises  and urgent care needs happening  managing the needs of a locality  population for a tiered approach  this is thinking about how we provide a  support on a local population level  for example benfleet or canby or oxford  to meet a breadth of needs  this idea can be illustrated in a  pyramid whereby the level of need is  inversely proportional to the size of  the population  at the top of the pyramid are the people  who have the highest needs  who need health and social care and a  whole host of services  and wrap around support then there are  those who have ongoing  care needs this could be people who may  be frail  or have one or more long-term condition  and need regular interface with their gp  and or hospital services then there are  people with urgent care needs where  people  have a health event or health crisis  such as a stroke  and need a lot of support for a short  period of time  the idea is that if the urgent care need  is met effectively  they can move into the bottom whole  population tier of the pyramid  sarah mills explained that the bottom  population tier is where community  resilience comes in  it really focuses on that prevention  agenda supporting people to take healthy  behaviors  making sure that planning decisions are  mindful of health and well-being needs  health promoting environments and  infrastructures  such as active travel for example she  added that this  is where this element of the health and  well-being strategy really focuses  on that whole population asset-based  prevention and creating healthy  environments for people to lead healthy  lives within  that leadership model could help to  drive this and make sure that all  partners are working together  so that a breadth of need can be met  sarah mills then asked the group for  their thoughts on this kind of model  and how the respective local  organizations could play a part to bring  that into being  the key things for this discussion was  sustainability of the community  partnership approach and isolation  ben brook started off this discussion  saying there is something around  coordination and where that coordination  takes place is something we need to get  to the bottom of  is the gp surgery the first place people  go when they want to talk to somebody  i think it's about community sector  navigation to understand what that local  offer is  and what each organization does perhaps  not initially  but since lockdown there is a lot more  communication between the community and  voluntary organizations  which was really powerful julie sulfur  continued the discussion by saying  it's not just health it's wherever  anybody goes to get some support  with families it might be the school or  people with preschoolers the family hubs  even if it's just a poster on the wall  in the library  julie sawford added that there's also an  element of can we rely on  smaller organizations to always be there  as a partnership we're really strong and  only getting stronger  so the partnership is what is able to  support the community  rather than the individual organizations  within that  victoria mazuki continued the  conversation saying i think it's about  getting the message out to places where  people go  people don't want the help people don't  want to ask for help  but people need the help places where  people go where they will get the  information  but they don't have to find it or make  it more obvious people usually only  approach a voluntary sector when in  crisis  or when they already know about them  lindsey gillingham  added this saying someone has to be  ready to accept help  we've got to be mindful of not having  too much information out in the  community  so people don't get confused she  continued by saying  as a cvs we've already got a membership  base  if we can't personally help we know  someone who can the strength  is our partnership work and the  collaborative working  other factors to acknowledge are wider  determinants of health and health  inequalities for example built a natural  environment work vulnerability income  crime education and skills direct and  indirect effects of covering 19 for  example end of furlough scheme and the  potential rise in  unemployment debt family tensions  housing evictions cognitive decline lack  of social interaction  plus mobilizing resources again in the  event of a second wave  next steps as a group is there other  data you can share to inform and provide  more insight  does anyone else need to be involved we  will meet as a subgroup between now and  december to continue our discussions  develop the narrative around this  priority and start thinking about some  actions  how we are tackling the problems and  what other actions would help us to  address them in terms of early  intervention and prevention  and think about assets within the castle  point and rockford

